  "strategy": "<h2>5. Rosebrock-like Correlation Barrier Construction (RBCC)</h2>\n\n<dt>5.1 Approach and rationale</dt>\n<dd>\n<p>Rather than hill climbing from known points, we'll construct a function whose autocorrelation properties are analyzed analytically and optimized. This method follows the principle of parameterized function forms that can be both visualized and globally optimized. We will use:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>A family of piecewise functions defined on [-1,1] (mapped to [0,2])</li>\n<li>Parameterized by two key parameters: a shape factor \u03b1 and a transition width \u03b2</li>\n<li>An analysis of the maximum correlation that must occur due to symmetry properties</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>This approach explicitly incorporates the knowledge of the problem's integer optimal solutions and their correlation properties. By working with slices and derivatives, we avoid purely numerical methods and use mathematical relationships to accelerate optimization.</p>\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.2 Mathematical specification</dt>\n<dd>\n<dt>2.1 Representation</dt>\n<dd>\nThe function is represented as a piecewise corresponds to samples we'll denote as:\n\n$$f(t) = \\begin{cases}\ng_1(-t) + g_1(t) & -1 \\leq t \\leq 1 \\\\\ng_1(-t) + g_1(t-2) & 1 < t \\leq 2\n\\end{cases}$$\n\nWhere $g_1(x) = \\exp(-\u03b1x^2)$. This defines a function that is symmetric about t=0 and has two humps.\n\nWe sample at n equally spaced points: $x_i = (i - 0.5) \\cdot \u0394x$, $\u0394x = 2/n$\n\nThe condition \u222bh dx=1 is naturally satisfied at a critical value of \u03b1.\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>2.2 Objective</dt>\n<dd>\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem requires:\n\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^{2-k} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$$\n\nAfter appropriate analysis and substitution, we convert this into:\n\n$$\\min_{\u03b1,\u03b2} \\left\\{ C = \\max_{|k|\u22642} \\left[ A(\u03b1,\u03b2,k) + B(\u03b1,\u03b2,k) + R(\u03b1,\u03b2,k) \\right] \\right\\}$$\n\nWhere A, B, R are analytical expressions derived from the correlation function using symmetry properties.\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>2.3 Formulation</dt>\n<dd>\nWe parameterize the function by two parameters:\n$$parameters = [\u03b1, \u03b3]$$\n\nWhere:\n- $\u03b1$ controls the global scaling and heaviness of the hump\n- $\u03b3$ controls the transition width between the two humps\n\nThe optimization problem is:\n$$\\min_{\u03b1,\u03b3} \\quad f(\u03b1,\u03b3) = \\max_k \\left\\{ \\text{Conv}_k(\u03b1,\u03b3) \\right\\} \\cdot \\Delta x$$\n\nWhere the individual correlation terms are expressed in terms of complete elliptic integrals and connected to the parameters.\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.3 Implementation procedure</dt>\n<dd>\n\n<dt>5.3.1 Parameter preparation</dt>\n<dd>\n1. Define parameters: \n   - $\u03b1 \\in [0.5, 5.0]$\n   - $\u03b3 \\in [2.0, 10.0]$\n\n2. Transformation to 360/720 points:\n   - $f(t) = \\exp(-\u03b1|t|) + \\exp(-\u03b1(t-2)^2)$ for the two-hump model\n\n3. Validation check for integral=1:\n\n   $$\\int_0^2 f(t) dt = \\sqrt{\\pi/\u03b1} + \\text{adjustment term}$$\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.3.2 Function evaluation</dt>\n<dd>\n1. Compute the autocorrelation using analytic expressions for the function's properties\n\n2. Handle the maximum value detection and the corresponding correlation:\n\n   $$\\text{Conv}(k) = \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx$$\n\n3. Implement:\n\n   from scipy.special import ellipe, ellipk\n   import numpy as np\n\n   def objective(alpha, gamma, n):\n      \"\"\"Full objective evaluation using the two-hump model\"\"\"\n      # Parameter mapping to grid\n      # ... omitted for conciseness ...\n      # Property extraction\n      # ... omitted ...\n\n      # Calculate critical correlation values\n      # ... omitted ...\n\n      # Spectral analysis to return min correlation\n      return final_c, None\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.3.3 Optimization sections</dt>\n<dd>\n1. **Hill Climbing Stage**  \n   - Gradient-free optimization using finite differences  \n   - Update rule: alpha_new = alpha + step * sign(gradient)\n\n2. **Global Exploration**  \n   - Multi-start optimization over alpha in [1.0, 4.0]  \n   - Simulated annealing between gamma boundaries\n\n3. **Finalization**  \n   - Phase transformation to 360 samples  \n   - Ensure normalization: h_sum = np.sum(h)  \n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.3.4 Timescale management</dt>\n<dd>\n1. 0.3 * budget_s: Initialization and gradient precomputation\n\n2. 0.4 * budget_s: \n   - Do 3 hill climbing runs\n   - Each with 450 iterations\n   - With random initialization\n\n3. 0.1 * budget_s: Finalization and parameter sweeps\n\n4. 0.2 * budget_s: Upsampling to 720 if time permits\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.3.5 Fallback strategy</dt>\n<dd>\nIf time runs out before reaching budget:\n   return parent state if available, else:\n   h = np.concatenate(\n        360/7  # exactly! then use fine grid\n   )\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.4 Search choices and useful variations</dt>\n<dd>\n\n<dt>5.4.1 Trust Region Management</dt>\n<dt>5.4.2 Dimensionality Control</dt>\n<dt>5.4.3 Adaptive Sweeps</dt>\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.5 Compute budget and robustness</dt>\n<dd>\nApproximate timings:\n   n=360: 5-7 seconds per evaluation\n   budget_s=1100 seconds = ~123-154 evaluations\n\nImplementation Choose from scipy.optimize. The objective function will be evaluated periodically to correctly bracket the global minimum.\n\n</dd>\n\n<dt>5.6 Validation and return contract</dt>\n<dd>\n\n<ol>\n1. random parameter and response validation:\n\n```python\ndef validate_function():\n    \"\"\"A simple mathematical consistency check\"\"\"\n    alpha = 1.5\n    gamma = 2.2\n    \n    # Fined-geometric checks omitted\n    assert np.isclose(integral(alpha), 0.998, rtol=0.02)\n```\n\n2. Dimension check:\n\n```python\ndef setup_fallback(n=360):\n    x = np.linspace(0, 2, n)\n    return np.exp(-0.5 * (x - 1)**2) / (np.max(np.exp(-0.5 * (x - 1)**2)) + np.exp(-0.5 * (x - 1)**2))\n```\n\n</ol>\n</dd>",
